From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:44:38 +0200
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/1] sched/fair: sched_entity layout for the EEVDF
 tree walk

pick_eevdf() and the augmented rbtree callbacks read run_node, vruntime,
min_vruntime, slice, min_slice and max_slice of every entity they visit.
On x86_64 these currently span three cachelines of sched_entity. This
RFC reorders sched_entity so the walk reads one line per entity (two
for the augment callback), without changing its size, the alignment of
avg, or any task_struct offset, and without increasing the number of
lines touched by any other access pattern checked (update_curr, PELT,
enqueue/dequeue, set_next_entity, detach_tasks, set_task_cpu, and
update_curr_common for rt/dl/scx/stop), on x86_64 and i386, with and
without FAIR_GROUP_SCHED.

It is an RFC because the evidence is static: a line count from pahole
offsets and hand-derived access lists. It has not been booted or
measured. Comments on whether this kind of reorder is wanted at all,
and on what measurement you would want to see, are the point of
posting it.

  #  summary                         impact            evidence
  1  reorder sched_entity for the    low: CFS pick     static analysis
     EEVDF tree walk                 and enqueue with  only; not
                                     many runnable     measured
                                     tasks per CPU

Lines touched per entity, before -> after (64-byte lines, absolute
task_struct offsets, x86_64 / i386 L1_CACHE_SHIFT=6 / i386 M686,
FAIR_GROUP_SCHED=y):

  pick_eevdf() heap search, per entity visited  3->1  2->1  3->2
  min_vruntime_update(), per entity             3->2  2->2  3->2
  update_curr() on a task                       4->3  3->3  3->3
  detach_tasks(), per task scanned              3->2  4->3  3->2
  enqueue / dequeue of a task                   5->4  4->4  4->4

Access lists used (fields of sched_entity; from the named functions):
  pick_eevdf:        run_node vruntime min_vruntime
  insert walk:       run_node deadline
  augment callback:  run_node vruntime slice min_vruntime min_slice
                     max_slice
  update_curr task:  exec_start sum_exec_runtime my_q vruntime h_load
                     deadline custom_slice slice vprot on_rq parent
                     cfs_rq
  update_curr_common: exec_start sum_exec_runtime my_q
  PELT:              avg on_rq sched_delayed my_q load
                     (+ runnable_weight for a group entity)
  enqueue task:      custom_slice slice h_load vlag vruntime
                     rel_deadline deadline avg on_rq sched_delayed
                     my_q load group_node exec_start cfs_rq parent
                     min_vruntime min_slice max_slice run_node
  dequeue task:      as enqueue minus custom_slice rel_deadline
                     exec_start deadline
  set_next_entity:   on_rq avg load my_q exec_start sum_exec_runtime
                     prev_sum_exec_runtime run_node min_vruntime
                     min_slice max_slice deadline slice vruntime h_load
                     vprot
  detach_tasks:      group_node sched_delayed exec_start cfs_rq avg
  set_task_cpu:      avg nr_migrations cfs_rq parent depth

Testing:
- pahole on kernel/sched/core.o before and after, five configurations:
  x86_64 and i386 (L1_CACHE_SHIFT 6 and M686) with FAIR_GROUP_SCHED,
  x86_64 and i386 M686 without. sizeof(struct sched_entity) and
  sizeof(struct task_struct) unchanged in all five; no task_struct
  member other than se's contents changes cacheline.
- Compiled kernel/sched/{core,fair,build_policy,build_utility}.o,
  kernel/fork.o and init/init_task.o for x86_64, i386, and x86_64
  without FAIR_GROUP_SCHED: no new warnings. fair.o text is 97 bytes
  smaller on x86_64 (bloat-o-meter).
- checkpatch.pl --strict: clean apart from the missing Signed-off-by.
- Checked on the built kernel with and without the patch: 10 access
  paths touch fewer lines, 3 the same, none more; pick_eevdf() 3 -> 1
  line per entity visited.

Not tested:
- Not booted on any architecture; no runtime measurement of any kind.
- No perf c2c / HITM data (the test machine exposes no memory-event
  PMU).
- 128-byte cacheline architectures (powerpc64, s390) and other 32-bit
  architectures were not built.
- No full kernel build; only the objects listed above.

The next test planned is an interleaved A/B on one CPU with N runnable
CFS tasks (N up to 512), counting kernel instructions, cycles and
L1-dcache misses per context switch. The recommendation changes to a
non-RFC posting only if L1 misses per switch fall with the patch as N
grows and nothing else regresses.

Tool assistance: the change, changelog and analysis in this series were
produced with Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) as a coding assistant,
prompted to find and fix cacheline placement problems in the scheduler
and to verify them with pahole. The patch carries an Assisted-by tag.

